					{
						"tools": [],
						"text": "Yes. The maestro player does this. You store a chord once, and then any single key plays that chord, shifted up or down to follow the key.\n\n1. `shift + player` opens the player list. Keep `shift` held and turn `E1` to maestro. When you let go of `shift`, the maestro page opens.\n2. Press `player` to switch the player on. The first press only shows the page, dimmed under an off box.\n3. Hold `shift` and play every note of the chord on the keyboard. That stores it.\n4. Release `shift`. Each key you press now plays the whole chord.\n\nOn the maestro page, `E1` sets roll (a strum from block chord to slow spread). `E2` sets the strum order, and `E4` holds the chord after you let go.\n\nMaestro works on sequenced notes too, so you can put a single note on each step and get a chord progression.\n\n[players.maestro] [players.overview]"
					}
